114
00:09:08.209 --> 00:09:09.083
Part 8.

115
00:09:09.363 --> 00:09:10.473
Common mistakes.

116
00:09:11.723 --> 00:09:18.016
Using a hash of the URL (MD5 truncated to 7 characters) without handling collisions.

117
00:09:18.296 --> 00:09:21.645
With billions of URLs, birthday collisions are certain.

118
00:09:21.925 --> 00:09:24.379
A single global counter as a bottleneck.

119
00:09:24.659 --> 00:09:28.947
Hand each node a block of ids (for example 1,000) at a time.

120
00:09:29.227 --> 00:09:35.008
Not validating the target URL, which turns the service into an open-redirect and phishing tool.

121
00:09:35.288 --> 00:09:37.379
Always returning 301.

122
00:09:37.659 --> 00:09:40.923
Browsers cache it permanently, so you lose click analytics.

123
00:09:41.203 --> 00:09:43.869
Use 302 if you need to count clicks.

124
00:09:45.219 --> 00:09:46.115
Part 9.

125
00:09:46.395 --> 00:09:47.739
Follow-up questions.

126
00:09:48.989 --> 00:09:49.864
Question.

127
00:09:50.144 --> 00:09:52.939
How do you scale id generation across regions?

128
00:09:53.219 --> 00:09:54.584
Thirty-second answer:

129
00:09:54.864 --> 00:10:03.867
Give each region or node its own range (a range allocator in ZooKeeper or a DB), or use Snowflake-style ids (problem 5).

130
00:10:04.147 --> 00:10:12.189
Base62 of a 64-bit Snowflake id is about 11 characters, which is longer but needs no coordination.

131
00:10:13.539 --> 00:10:14.414
Question.

132
00:10:14.694 --> 00:10:16.678
How would you expire links and clean up?

133
00:10:16.958 --> 00:10:18.323
Thirty-second answer:

134
00:10:18.603 --> 00:10:19.862
Store expiresat.

135
00:10:20.142 --> 00:10:23.939
The redirect path checks it (and the cache TTL is capped at it).

136
00:10:24.219 --> 00:10:29.937
A background job or DynamoDB or Cassandra TTL deletes expired rows.

137
00:10:30.217 --> 00:10:34.675
Don't reuse codes for a long time after they expire, to avoid confusion.

